                  "most states; private and deemed-college fees run into lakhs per "
                  "year. Before counselling season, sit with your family and decide "
                  "the financial ceiling — a seat you cannot pay for is not a seat."))
S += chapter(8, "Seats, Quotas and What a Score Buys", c8)

# ============================================================== CH 9
c9 = []
c9.append(P("The NMC-notified syllabus is the NCERT Class 11 and 12 syllabus of "
            "Physics, Chemistry and Biology, published as Appendix-III of the NTA "
            "bulletin. 'Covering the syllabus' is therefore a bounded, finishable "
            "job — roughly 100 chapters across two years of school. What separates "
            "scores is not which chapters you touched, but how many times you "
            "returned to the high-yield ones.", lead))
c9.append(P("Where the marks live", h2))
c9.append(mk_table(
    ["Subject", "Questions", "Marks", "How it behaves"],
    [["Biology", "90", "360", "Direct NCERT lines, diagrams, exceptions — factual recall plus careful reading"],
     ["Chemistry", "45", "180", "Physical (calculation), Organic (mechanism and reactions), Inorganic (memory)"],
     ["Physics", "45", "180", "Concept plus numerical — the rank-shifter for serious candidates"]],
    [0.18, 0.16, 0.13, 0.53]))
c9.append(P("Within each subject, five-year analyses of previous papers by coaching "
            "platforms (Careers360, Physics Wallah, Class24 and others) find a "
            "stable pattern: a third of the chapters carry roughly two-thirds of the "
            "questions. The subject playbooks in Chapters 10 to 12 name those "
            "chapters with their typical question counts, so 'high-yield' here "
            "means 'consistently asked over the last five years', not a leaked "
            "promise."))
c9.append(P("The study order that works", h2))
c9.append(numbered([
    "Finish a chapter's NCERT reading first — every line, including captions, examples and the summary points.",
    "Solve the previous-year questions from that chapter the same week, untimed, to check comprehension.",
    "Add one layer beyond NCERT only where the PYQs show it is needed (a reference book for numericals in Physics, extra practice for Organic).",
    "Return to the chapter on a 1 - 7 - 30 rhythm: re-read and re-solve within a week, then test it again within a month.",
]))
c9.append(P("Do not study subject blocks sequentially — 'Biology till December, then "
            "Physics' is how January becomes a panic. Rotate all three subjects every "
            "week, because NEET rewards breadth held simultaneously."))
S += chapter(9, "The Syllabus, Sorted", c9)
S.append(PageBreak())

# ============================================================== CH 10
c10 = []
c10.append(P("Biology is half the paper — 90 questions, 360 marks — and it is the "
            "most NCERT-faithful section: in a typical year, most Biology questions "
            "can be answered from lines that exist in the NCERT textbook, if you "
            "have read them carefully enough times. If your Biology is strong, the "
            "paper is a reading test; if it is weak, no amount of Physics heroics "
            "can buy back 360 marks.", lead))
c10.append(mk_table(
    ["High-yield area", "Typical questions (5-yr avg.)", "Priority"],
    [["Human Physiology (all chapters)", "10 - 12", "Highest"],
     ["Genetics and Evolution (Principles + Molecular Basis)", "8 - 10", "Highest"],
     ["Biotechnology (both chapters)", "5 - 6", "Highest"],
     ["Ecology and Environment", "5 - 6", "Highest"],
     ["Cell biology (unit of life + cell cycle)", "6 - 8", "High"],
     ["Plant Physiology", "5 - 6", "High"],
     ["Reproduction (plants + human)", "5 - 6", "High"],
     ["Animal Kingdom and structural organisation", "6 - 8", "High"]],
    [0.44, 0.32, 0.24]))
c10.append(P("At the single-chapter level, Molecular Basis of Inheritance (about 7 "
            "questions a year on the five-year average), Biotechnology: Principles "
            "and Processes (about 6), and Human Health and Disease are the "
            "perennial heavyweights. One chapter — Human Physiology as a block — "
            "alone outweighs any three Physics chapters combined."))
c10.append(P("How to actually study it", h2))
c10.append(bullets([
    "Read NCERT with a pen, not a highlighter: convert every paragraph into one recall question in your own words.",
    "Learn every NCERT diagram until you can redraw and label it from memory — pedigree analysis, DNA replication, the human heart, the nephron, the menstrual cycle, ecological pyramids.",
    "Keep an exceptions notebook: NEET's Biology traps are NCERT's fine print (which hormone is not a peptide, which algae stores floridean starch, which vessel carries oxygenated blood in the fetus).",
    "Recite the summary points of each chapter aloud before sleeping — recall practice, not re-reading, is what moves factual content into long-term memory.",
]))
c10.append(callout("THE 300-GATE",
                  "A realistic first target for serious aspirants is 300+ of 360 in "
                  "Biology (75+ of 90 correct, at most a handful wrong). Every mark "
                  "you drop here must be earned back in Physics, where marks are "
                  "four times harder to come by."))
S += chapter(10, "The Biology Playbook (360 Marks)", c10)

# ============================================================== CH 11
c11 = []
c11.append(P("Chemistry is 45 questions and, for most candidates, the highest "
            "marks-per-hour subject on the paper: a large share of its questions are "
            "one-step recall or one-line application. It behaves as three different "
            "subjects — train them separately.", lead))
c11.append(mk_table(
    ["Branch", "Typical questions", "Character"],
    [["Physical Chemistry", "15 - 18", "Calculations: mole concept, equilibrium, thermodynamics, electrochemistry, kinetics, solutions"],
     ["Organic Chemistry", "13 - 16", "Mechanisms, name reactions, conversions and GOC logic"],
     ["Inorganic Chemistry", "12 - 15", "NCERT memory: periodicity, bonding, p / d / f block, coordination compounds"]],
    [0.24, 0.22, 0.54]))
c11.append(P("High-yield chapters by five-year averages: Aldehydes, Ketones and "
            "Carboxylic Acids (about 4 questions a year), basic principles of Organic "
            "Chemistry (about 3), Hydrocarbons (about 3), Chemical Bonding and "
            "Molecular Structure (about 3), Coordination Compounds (about 3), "
            "Equilibrium (about 3) and Chemical Kinetics (about 3), with Biomolecules "
            "and Chemistry in Everyday Life as easy, finishable marks."))
c11.append(P("The three-branch method", h2))
c11.append(bullets([
    "Physical: keep a formula-on-one-page sheet per chapter and practise numericals daily until units and approximations are reflex. Most lost marks are arithmetic, not concepts.",
    "Organic: master GOC and reaction mechanisms once, properly — then every named reaction becomes a consequence rather than a memory load. Write each mechanism out by hand at least twice.",
    "Inorganic: this is straight NCERT line-by-line territory, like Biology. Revise it weekly with your exceptions notebook; never leave it for a single 'final month' pass.",
]))
S += chapter(11, "The Chemistry Playbook (180 Marks)", c11)

# ============================================================== CH 12
c12 = []
c12.append(P("Physics decides ranks, because it is the section half the field "
            "fears. The syllabus is NCERT-based but the questions are numericals "
            "in disguise: formulas are easy to memorise and useless until you can "
            "deploy them on an unseen two-step problem in ninety seconds.", lead))
c12.append(mk_table(
    ["Block", "Typical questions", "Key chapters"],
    [["Mechanics", "13 - 15", "Kinematics, laws of motion, work-energy, rotation, gravitation, properties of matter"],
     ["Electrodynamics", "8 - 10", "Current Electricity (about 4 / yr, the single highest-yield chapter), electrostatics, magnetism, EMI and AC"],
     ["Optics", "5 - 6", "Ray Optics (about 3 / yr), Wave Optics"],
     ["Modern Physics and Semiconductors", "5 - 6", "Dual nature, atoms and nuclei, semiconductors"],
     ["Heat, waves and oscillations", "5 - 6", "Thermodynamics and KTG, oscillations, waves"]],
    [0.28, 0.18, 0.54]))
c12.append(P("How to actually study it", h2))
c12.append(bullets([
    "Derivations first, formulas second: if you can re-derive a result, you cannot be defeated by a rearranged version of it.",
    "Numericals daily, from day one — 20 focused problems beat 100 passive solved examples. Time yourself loosely (two minutes a problem) so speed grows without panic.",
    "Maintain a conditions notebook: every formula with the conditions under which it holds (ideal gas, constant acceleration, thin lens, small angle). Half of NEET Physics tricks are broken assumptions.",
    "Practise error-analysis on every wrong numerical: was it the concept, the algebra, or the units?",
]))
c12.append(P("A note on Calculus: NEET does not require it, but comfort with basic "
            "algebraic manipulation and ratios is non-negotiable. If Class 11 Maths "
            "fundamentals are shaky, spend two weeks on fractions, indices, "
            "trigonometric ratios and graph-reading — it pays across Physics and "
            "Physical Chemistry."))
S += chapter(12, "The Physics Playbook (180 Marks)", c12)
S.append(PageBreak())

